{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for the deployment note at exact clean head df27e17c51d35bf4297584923aef88a767c8102d. Independent checks: git diff --check, make vet, and make test pass; the worktree is clean. The prior atomic-claim and schedule-posture findings are closed, and length-prefixing the four fields is injective. Three issues remain. F1 MUST FIX: the note defines bytes(key) as the exact raw octets the submitter sent and calls the round-trip total, but the actual signed intent stores IdempotencyKey as a canonical-CBOR text string; the drain sees the decoded string, not original JSON or transport octets, and validation rejects empty values plus CR, LF, and NUL. Define bytes(key) as the UTF-8 encoding of the canonical decoded intent text with those admissibility constraints. The empty-string case may test the standalone encoder or ref generator, but cannot be described as a valid candidate intent. F2 MUST FIX: case 2b still cannot prove pending-run replacement with only two overlapping runs. GitHub concurrency permits one running and one pending run; replacement requires three overlapping runs: one remains running, a second becomes pending, and a third replaces the second. The survivor must then drain all candidates. F3 BLOCKER: the mirror is a useful named durable replica, but its update protocol spans the publication and control repositories and is not atomic. If the mirror advances first and publication fails, an ordinary crash is misclassified as vandalism; if publication advances first and mirror update fails before the claim is released, a rewind to the stale mirror can evade detection. Specify the publish/mirror/claim-release order, terminal handling and recovery for each partial success, retain the claim until both durable states are reconciled, and require the control mirror to be protected and verified before advancement rather than treated as unquestioned truth. What held: the explicit length-prefix construction closes boundary ambiguity; the corrected five-minute-plus-unbounded schedule posture and public auto-disable warning are honest; backlog drain is now separate; cases 1, 3-5, 7-8, 10 otherwise remain observable; and case 6 correctly separates automatic detection from deliberate operator recovery.","body":{"commit":"df27e17c51d35bf4297584923aef88a767c8102d","head":"df27e17c51d35bf4297584923aef88a767c8102d","verdict":"changes-requested"}}